Patch: make PARSEC facesim buildable and runnable under gem5 SE-mode
Applies to: parsec-3.0/pkgs/apps/facesim/src/Public_Library/Read_Write/FILE_UTILITIES.cpp
(source: https://github.com/cirosantilli/parsec-benchmark, tag 3.0)

Root cause: Create_Directory() shells out via system("mkdir -p ...") to
create its output directory. gem5 SE-mode syscall emulation does not
support the fork()+exec() sequence a real system() call requires; the
guest process decodes garbage after the call and gem5 panics
("Tried to read unmapped address ..."). Directory_Exists() was also
broken independent of this (std::ifstream on a directory path fails to
open on Linux, so it always returned false and system() always ran,
even when the directory already existed).

Fix: replace both with direct stat()/mkdir() syscalls -- no subprocess,
no shell involved. This is a real behavioral difference (the function no
longer just runs `mkdir -p` and inherits its shell semantics) but the
recursive mkdir() loop below reproduces the same effect.

Apply with:
  cd parsec-3.0/pkgs/apps/facesim/src
  patch -p1 < /path/to/facesim-gem5-se-mode.patch

Then build per patches/README.md in this repo before running under gem5.

--- a/Public_Library/Read_Write/FILE_UTILITIES.cpp
+++ b/Public_Library/Read_Write/FILE_UTILITIES.cpp
@@ -99,15 +99,24 @@
 
 bool Directory_Exists (const std::string& dirname)
 {
-	return !!std::ifstream (dirname.c_str());
+	struct stat st;
+	return stat (dirname.c_str(), &st) == 0 && S_ISDIR (st.st_mode);
 }
 
 bool Create_Directory (const std::string& dirname, bool exit_on_fail)
 {
 	if (!Directory_Exists (dirname))
 	{
-		std::string command = STRING_UTILITIES::string_sprintf ("mkdir -p %s", dirname.c_str());
-		std::cerr << "Creating directory using system(\"" << command << "\")...";
-		system (command.c_str());
+		// mkdir -p via direct mkdir() calls, one path component at a time --
+		// avoid system()/fork()+exec(), which gem5 SE-mode can't emulate.
+		std::cerr << "Creating directory \"" << dirname << "\"...";
+		std::string path;
+		size_t pos = 0;
+		while (pos != std::string::npos)
+		{
+			pos = dirname.find ('/', pos + 1);
+			path = (pos == std::string::npos) ? dirname : dirname.substr (0, pos);
+			if (!path.empty()) mkdir (path.c_str(), 0755);
+		}
 
 		if (!Directory_Exists (dirname))
